    "result_text": "Build succeeds; the suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `customerCode` assignment (`<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) at owner creation, keeping structural impact low by adding small, self-contained units rather than growing any existing method or class.\n\nChanges:\n- **`AssignCustomerCode.java`** (new) \u2014 a one-method function that reads the built `Owner`, computes `<upper first 3 of lastName>-<count+1 zero-padded>` via `OwnerRepository.findAll().size() + 1`, and sets it. No surrounding complexity.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `assignCustomerCode` step between `build` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance, so the sequence is read inside the create transaction (owner not yet persisted \u2192 `count + 1`).\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `customer_code` column field with a plain getter/setter (CC 1 each); MapStruct auto-maps it to the response DTO by name, so no mapper change.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 declared the read-only `customerCode` property on the `Owner` response schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n- **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql` / `data.sql`** \u2014 added the `customer_code VARCHAR(20)` column (HSQLDB schema is authoritative for tests; other profiles use `ddl-auto=update`) and padded the 10 positional seed inserts with a trailing `NULL`.\n\nSince the sequence is stored at creation, the two owners in the test get consecutive numbers (`SMI-000X`, then `JON-000(X+1)`) that persist and read back correctly via `GET`.",
